# Host-side components of the delivery stand, and where they belong.
#
# This file is the single source both install-ci-host.sh and
# verify-deployment.sh read, so an installer that puts a file somewhere and a
# verifier that looks for it elsewhere cannot drift apart.
#
# Columns: repository path, deployed path, mode, owner, group.
#
# Deployed copies do not update themselves from git. Before this manifest
# existed they were scp'd by hand, which is how /usr/local/sbin came to hold
# mirror-health-check next to mirror-health-check-v2, four dated backups of it,
# and a pipeline that ran whichever one a cron file happened to name.

ci/host/gatebsd-ci-build                     /usr/local/libexec/gatebsd-ci-build              0750 root wheel
ci/host/gatebsd-ci-dispatch                  /usr/local/libexec/gatebsd-ci-dispatch           0550 root operator
ci/host/prepare-gatebsd-build-jail.sh        /usr/local/libexec/prepare-gatebsd-build-jail.sh 0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/verify-deployment.sh        /usr/local/libexec/verify-deployment.sh          0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/leftovers.sh                /usr/local/libexec/leftovers.sh                  0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/retire-leftovers.sh         /usr/local/libexec/retire-leftovers.sh           0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/deployment.scan             /usr/local/libexec/deployment.scan               0644 root wheel

# The key the appliance verifies this stand's package repository with. The build
# jail now verifies it with the same key, so the key had to stop being a file
# that only existed in the repository and in whatever copy a machine happened to
# hold.
etc/pkg/keys/gatebsd-stand.pub               /usr/local/etc/pkg/keys/gatebsd-stand.pub        0644 root wheel

infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-sync-freebsd-15-amd64.sh /usr/local/sbin/mirror-sync-freebsd-15-amd64 0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-health-check.sh      /usr/local/sbin/mirror-health-check              0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-publish-http.sh      /usr/local/sbin/mirror-publish-http              0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-publish-release.sh   /usr/local/sbin/mirror-publish-release           0750 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-status-update.sh     /usr/local/sbin/mirror-status-update             0750 root wheel
# 0755, unlike its neighbours, and deliberately: the CI dispatcher execs it as
# ci_deployer, which is in group operator, not wheel. It only reads the
# published status files, so there is nothing here to withhold.
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-status-report.sh     /usr/local/sbin/mirror-status-report             0755 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ci-dashboard-update.sh      /usr/local/sbin/ci-dashboard-update              0755 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/renew-stand-tls.sh          /usr/local/sbin/renew-stand-tls                 0750 root wheel

# The crontab that schedules all of the above. It belongs here for the same
# reason the scripts do, and for one more: cron(8) reads every file in a cron.d
# directory whatever it is named, so an older generation left beside this one
# is a second live schedule, not a backup. Two such copies were found here --
# one of them ran an unmanaged mirror-health-check-v2 that wrote the working
# checker's readiness flag. Listing the file makes both its content and its
# leftovers subject to verify-deployment.sh and retire-leftovers.sh.
infra/freebsd-ci/freebsd-mirror.cron         /usr/local/etc/cron.d/freebsd-local-repo        0644 root wheel

# The appliance's package set, and the script that rebuilds the local
# repository from it. Both were heredocs inside
# ci/host/configure_freebsd_mirror.sh, which is not part of this mechanism and
# never was: it is the one-shot poudriere bring-up.
#
# That made the crontab above a file with two writers. The manifest won the
# race -- the deployed schedule is this repository's five entries -- but only
# because nobody re-ran the script. A re-run would have replaced the whole
# schedule with the single 04:15 line the heredoc carried, dropping the FreeBSD
# mirror sync, the health check, the status update and the TLS renewal, and
# verify-deployment.sh would have said so only afterwards.
#
# The pkglist lost the same race the other way. Its live copy had been edited
# by hand to lang/python312, databases/py-sqlite3@py312 and the two amnezia
# ports the router's VPN runs on; the heredoc still said lang/python3 and knew
# nothing about amnezia. Nothing was watching, because the pkglist was in no
# manifest and no verifier looked at it. Listing it here is what makes a hand
# edit visible, and what retires the gatebsd-pkglist.before-amnezia-20260730
# sitting beside it.
infra/freebsd-ci/gatebsd-pkglist             /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/gatebsd-pkglist      0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/update-freebsd-local-repo.sh /usr/local/sbin/update-freebsd-local-repo      0750 root wheel

# poudriere's own configuration, and the same story a third time. It had three
# writers: bootstrap.sh installed infra/freebsd-ci/poudriere.conf over it
# wholesale, ci/host/configure_freebsd_mirror.sh rewrote eleven keys in place
# with awk, and a hand edit added nine more. Nobody was comparing, because the
# file was in no manifest -- so the repository's copy drifted into a fiction
# that had never been deployed, and installing it would have dropped
# PKG_REPO_SIGNING_KEY and left the stand publishing an unsigned repository
# that the router's pkg refuses on `signature_type: "pubkey"`.
#
# Listing it here is also what makes leftovers_beside() look in
# /usr/local/etc at all: three poudriere.conf.before-local-repo-20260714-*
# copies had been sitting next to the live file since July, invisible to
# retire-leftovers.sh for exactly that reason.
infra/freebsd-ci/poudriere.conf              /usr/local/etc/poudriere.conf                   0644 root wheel

# The two rc.d scripts the stand boots with, and the sudo rule the CI
# dispatcher runs under. All three were hand-installed and in no manifest, so
# the first thing that ever looked at them was the directory scan
# deployment.scan now declares.
#
# stand_network_ready is the reason the stand has a network at boot: it puts the
# address back and re-adds the default route if netif came up without them.
# Until 14 August 2026 it did that for a lagg and an address written in by hand,
# because the stand was a physical machine. That machine was decommissioned in
# August 2026 and its disk reformatted; the stand is a virtual machine with
# neither the lagg nor the address, and the script reads the interface, address
# and router out of rc.conf instead of knowing them. Proven by running it on
# FreeBSD with the address removed from a scratch interface: it put the address
# back.
#
# A row here deploys a file; it does not enable a service. Between the move to
# the VM and 16 August 2026 this script was installed, correct, and never ran:
# `stand_network_ready_enable` was in no rc.conf and the script's own default is
# NO, so `service ... enabled` answered no for weeks while this comment claimed
# the stand had a network at boot because of it. A manifest row is evidence that
# a file is in place, and of nothing else -- for an rc script, check `enabled`
# on the machine as well.
#
# It was called n100_network_ready until 16 August 2026, after the model of a
# machine that no longer exists. Renaming a deployed rc script is not a rename
# of one row: rcorder(8) reads every file in that directory whatever it is
# called, so the previous file had to be carried out of rc.d by the retirement
# pass and its `_enable` variable replaced in rc.conf. A leftover there is a
# second script running at boot, before anyone is watching.
#
# nginx_after_libvirt is gone with the same move. It ran no commands: it existed
# for its REQUIRE line, which kept nginx from starting before libvirtd created
# the bridge nginx bound to. The VM runs no libvirt and nginx no longer binds a
# bridge address -- it listens on every interface. An ordering script naming a
# service that will never exist is not harmless clutter here: rcorder(8) reads
# every file in that directory, and a rule about a missing provider is a rule
# nobody can check.
infra/freebsd-ci/rc.d-stand_network_ready    /usr/local/etc/rc.d/stand_network_ready         0755 root wheel
ci/host/ci_deployer.sudoers                  /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/ci_deployer            0440 root wheel
ci/host/wheel.sudoers                        /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/wheel                  0440 root wheel

# What lets poudriere build a pkgbase jail: the FreeBSD-base repository is
# disabled by default and the stand's jail is `-m pkgbase`. Thirty-one bytes,
# written by hand in July, and the whole build side of the stand stops working
# if it goes missing -- which is a poor reason for it to have been the one file
# in /usr/local/etc/pkg/repos that nothing was watching.
infra/freebsd-ci/pkg-repos-FreeBSD.conf      /usr/local/etc/pkg/repos/FreeBSD.conf           0644 root wheel

# mirror-sync-git was that known gap until 27 August 2026: scheduled by cron,
# no source here, unmanaged. It stopped being academic when the copy on the
# stand turned out to keep every previous version of every mirrored repository
# forever -- 104 of them, 180 GB, back to 19 July. The pool filled, the release
# mirror quietly stopped updating for four days, and nothing in this tree could
# even show what the script did. It is carried here now, with the pruning it
# always needed.
infra/freebsd-ci/mirror-sync-git.sh          /usr/local/sbin/mirror-sync-git                 0750 root wheel

# Log rotation for everything above. Absent entirely until 27 August 2026: five
# logs appending for ever, the busiest of them every three minutes, onto the
# root pool.
infra/freebsd-ci/newsyslog-gatebsd-stand.conf /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/gatebsd-stand.conf 0644 root wheel

# The stand's networking, which existed only on the stand until 27 August 2026.
#
# hn0 and hn1 are in the same subnet, so the machine needs two FIBs and a pf
# route-to rule to use both -- none of which rc.conf can express. All six files
# were live and none was in any manifest row: verify-deployment.sh reported the
# three under /usr/local as "files nothing accounts for", and the three in /etc
# were not reported at all, because its scan roots stop at /usr/local. A rebuilt
# stand would have come up on one leg without noticing the other was gone.
#
# pf is enabled here as a routing primitive, not as a firewall: pf.conf passes
# everything and says so in its own comment.
infra/freebsd-ci/hyperv_multihoming          /usr/local/etc/rc.d/hyperv_multihoming          0555 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/hyperv-failover-realtek     /usr/local/sbin/hyperv-failover-realtek         0555 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/hyperv-failover-wifi        /usr/local/sbin/hyperv-failover-wifi            0555 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/stand-pf.conf               /etc/pf.conf                                    0600 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/stand-pf.hyperv-primary.conf /etc/pf.hyperv-primary.conf                    0600 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/stand-pf.hyperv-wifi.conf   /etc/pf.hyperv-wifi.conf                        0600 root wheel
# The poudriere ports overlay.
#
# The stand's ports tree is a plain checkout of git.FreeBSD.org/ports.git, and
# `poudriere ports -u` throws away anything edited inside it. Python security
# backports therefore live here and are overlaid at build time. Amnezia ports
# deliberately have no local rows: official amnezia-kmod 3.1, amnezia-tools 3.1
# and amneziawg-go 3.1 provide both complete data paths.
#
# Registered once with:
#   poudriere ports -c -p gatebsd -m null -M /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay -f none
# and selected per build with `-O gatebsd`. NOT with OVERLAYS= in poudriere.conf:
# bulk.sh sources the config and then assigns OVERLAYS="" unconditionally, so a
# setting there is a silent no-op that builds the upstream version while looking
# configured. The tree name has no dash on purpose -- poudriere parses one as a
# SETNAME.

infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/Makefile                    /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/Makefile                    0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/Makefile.version            /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/Makefile.version            0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/pkg-descr                   /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/pkg-descr                   0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/pkg-plist                   /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/pkg-plist                   0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/patch-Lib-imaplib.py  /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/patch-Lib-imaplib.py  0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/patch-Lib-poplib.py   /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/patch-Lib-poplib.py   0644 root wheel
infra/freebsd-ci/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/pkg-message.in       /usr/local/share/gatebsd/ports-overlay/lang/python312/files/pkg-message.in       0644 root wheel
infra/forgejo/backup.sh                                                    /usr/local/sbin/gatebsd-forgejo-backup                                     0550 root wheel
infra/forgejo/forgejo-backup.cron                                          /usr/local/etc/cron.d/forgejo-backup                                       0644 root wheel

# Three rows left here on 14 August 2026 with the physical N100, and they are
# named rather than silently dropped because each was doing something on that
# machine that has no meaning on this one.
#
#   loader.conf         coretemp_load -- the Intel core temperature driver.
#                       A Hyper-V guest has no coretemp to load.
#   rc.conf.d-powerd    adaptive frequency scaling. The hypervisor host owns
#                       the CPU frequency; a guest asking for it changes
#                       nothing and runs a daemon for no reason.
#   sysctl.conf.local   dev.hwpstate_intel.{0..3}.epp=80 -- four cores' Intel
#                       energy-performance preference. The VM has two vCPUs and
#                       none of those sysctls exist, so this file turns into
#                       four errors at every boot.
#
# The last one is the reason this is a deletion rather than a "leave it, it is
# harmless": a config that errors at boot is exactly the kind of noise that
# teaches people to ignore boot output.
